.Dd August 4, 2026
.Dt DDI_NCPUS_EXPECTED 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ddi_ncpus_expected
.Nd obtain the expected number of CPUs in the system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunddi.h
.Ft uint_t
.Fo ddi_ncpus_expected
.Fa void
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos DDI specific
.Pq illumos DDI .
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn ddi_ncpus_expected
function returns the number of CPUs that the system is expected to have
once it has finished booting.
.Pp
Device drivers commonly size resources such as interrupt vectors, queues,
and worker threads based on the number of CPUs in the system.
A driver that attaches while the system is booting, such as the driver for
a boot device, may attach before the additional processors have been
started, at a time when only the boot processor is running.
Counting the running CPUs at that point would size such resources for a
single CPU.
While only the boot processor is running, the
.Fn ddi_ncpus_expected
function therefore returns the number of processors that the system is
expected to start during boot.
Once further processors are running, it returns the number of CPUs that
are present.
.Pp
The returned value is an estimate that is intended for sizing purposes.
The number of CPUs that are present can change over the lifetime of the
system
.Pq for example, through processor hotplug ,
and not every CPU that is present is necessarily online.
.Sh CONTEXT
The
.Fn ddi_ncpus_expected
function may be called from
.Sy user ,
.Sy kernel ,
or
.Sy interrupt
context.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Fn ddi_ncpus_expected
function always succeeds.
It returns the expected number of CPUs, which is never less than 1.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr attach 9E ,
.Xr ddi_intr_get_navail 9F
